  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1119 PM CDT THU SEP 10 2026\n\nThis evening, an upper shortwave continues to dig southwestward\nout of an upper low over  Eastern Canada and has deteriorated the\nridge across the Southern US. This will give just enough northwest\nflow to push a surface front close to Alabama to stretch from \nArkansas to Kentucky and the Maryland coast by early Friday as the\nparent associated surface low moves eastward across and out of \nQuebec. Rain chances increase significantly for Friday into the \nweekend for all of Central Alabama. The main energy from the \nsurface low will push eastward into the Atlantic on Friday with the\nboundary pushing back well to the north, giving way to a moist \nsoutherly fetch into the state. In the upper levels, positively \ntilted troughing shifts closer to Alabama with ample disturbance \nactivity expected for Friday and Saturday across Central Alabama. \nWhile the probability for widespread flooding is low, QPF amounts \nof 0.5 to around 1.5 inches are likely with up to 2 inches of rain\nmay occur over this time frame with a few locally isolated higher\namounts.\n\nBy Sunday, the trough weakens as the upper ridge builds across\nthe eastern half of the Southern US. While there is still a\nmedium chance for activity to occur, it should be more scattered\nin nature with lower amounts overall. By Monday, and into the\nmiddle part of next week, the upper ridge is expected to strengthen\nfurther across East Central Conus. This will yield a weak easterly\nflow into Central Alabama and more diurnally enhanced isolated \nconvective activity once again for the remainder of the forecast \nwith low QPF amounts.\n\n08",
